[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "Open Games (1...e5)"]
[Variation "Italian Game: Giuoco Pianissimo"]
[ECO "C54"]
[Character "solid,positional"]
[Risk "low"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "none"]
[Themes "castle-early,solid-centre"]
[Kind "main"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Defends e5 with a piece and develops. Black never plays 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 (the Stafford Gambit) or 3...Nd4 (the Blackburne Shilling): traps for beginners that lose against anyone who knows the answer. } 3. Bc4 3... Bc5 { Copies White: the bishop eyes f2 and Black can castle soon. It also avoids 3...Nf6 4.Ng5, which asks a beginner for several exact moves. }
4. c3 { Prepares d4 to build a big centre. } 4... Nf6 { Develops with an attack on e4, so White has to defend the pawn. }
5. d3 { A quiet reply that guards e4. }
5... d6 { Gives e5 a pawn to defend it and frees the c8 bishop. }
6. O-O 6... O-O { Castle now: the king is safe behind its pawns and the rook is ready for the centre. }
7. Re1 7... a6 { Makes room on a7, so the bishop can step back there if White plays d4. It also stops Bb5 or Nb5 ideas. }
8. Bb3 8... Ba7 { Keeps the bishop on the long diagonal, out of reach of a pawn on d4, where it still presses on f2. }
9. h3 9... h6 { Stops Bg5, which would pin the knight on f6 to the queen. }
10. Nbd2 10... Be6 { Develops the last minor piece and offers a trade for White's strong bishop. Plan: ...Re8, ...Qd7 and ...Rad8. If White plays d4, take with ...exd4 and recapture to keep the pieces active. } *
